---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import th1 from '../../assets/projects/th/th-1.png';
import th2 from '../../assets/projects/th/th-2.png';
import th3 from '../../assets/projects/th/th-3.png';
import th4 from '../../assets/projects/th/th-4.png';
import th5 from '../../assets/projects/th/th-5.png';
import th6 from '../../assets/projects/th/th-6.png';
import th7 from '../../assets/projects/th/th-7.png';
---

<Layout
  title="Personal Mark — Tyler Hilker"
  description="Making my own mark: a personal logo built from my initials, Highland tartan colors, and a love of architecture."
>
  <main>
    <header>
      <a href="/" class="back muted small">← Tyler Hilker</a>
      <h1>Making My Own Mark</h1>
      <p class="muted">There's more than a bit of vanity here, I'm not going to lie. I made a mark for myself out of my initials.</p>
    </header>

    <hr />

    <article>
      <Image src={th1} alt="Personal mark exploration" width={700} loading="lazy" />

      <p>
        For years, I've long found myself messing around with an initial-mark when initialing
        forms of some sort. The two pairs of perpendicular lines were begging for more attention.
      </p>
      <p>
        The mark &amp; scheme were inspired (knowingly &amp; subconsciously) by a few things. Most
        of these rationalizations are post-hoc: observations &amp; reflections that couldn't have
        been verbalized at the outset, but meaning emerges throughout the process. I think this is
        true of most brand/logo/art explanations, and I've tried to minimize pretense &amp;
        puffery in mine.
      </p>

      <h3>The characters themselves</h3>
      <p>
        The simple geometry is undeniable. Straight, confident strokes and right angles. They're
        almost as basic as type gets and they own it. Each of these explorations below had their
        beginning in sketchbooks &amp; mortgage paperwork.
      </p>

      <Image src={th2} alt="Initial-mark character explorations" width={700} loading="lazy" />

      <p>
        In the end, I gravitated toward simplified lines &amp; geometry. I had tried several
        iterations with thinner lines, but they stood too weak.
      </p>

      <Image src={th3} alt="Simplified lines and geometry" width={700} loading="lazy" />
      <Image src={th4} alt="Black, white, and size variations" width={700} loading="lazy" />
      <p class="caption muted small">Black, white, &amp; size variations</p>

      <h3>My Scottish lineage</h3>
      <p>
        Each side of my family has a significant dose of Highland heritage. Kilts, tartans, &amp;
        everything. I grew up watching the caber toss at various Highland festivals as my sister
        competed in the highland dance contests. I enjoyed bagpipe music.
      </p>
      <p>
        The color palette is pulled from one of my family tartans, the
        <a href="https://www.lochcarron.co.uk/macinnes-hunting-ancient-heavy-weight-tartan-fabric/">Macinnes Ancient Hunting</a>
        pattern. The top row of colors is what I've run with; the others were other
        considerations pulled from the fabric with a color picker.
      </p>

      <Image src={th5} alt="Macinnes Ancient Hunting tartan color palette" width={700} loading="lazy" />
      <Image src={th6} alt="Color palette applied to the mark" width={700} loading="lazy" />

      <p>
        Conveniently, the aforementioned 90-degree letterforms echo the criss-cross of tartan
        warp and weft.
      </p>

      <Image src={th7} alt="Tartan-inspired mark variations" width={700} loading="lazy" />

      <h3>Architecture</h3>
      <p>
        Since I was a kid, I've been messing around with architecture. CAD floorplans for fun,
        sketching landscapes in my spare time.
      </p>
      <p>
        My Instagram feed, an easy reference point for my architectural interests, consistently
        pulls from a few quasi-paradoxical themes: thoughtful/unexpected/clever design,
        Scandinavian simplicity, &amp; rugged, mountain cabins.
      </p>
      <p>
        I like that the mark evokes for me a mountaintop retreat: the stable, wide stance of the
        H &amp; its descending stems like downslope deck support posts. The outstretched arm of
        the T reaches out like a cantilevered roof, sheltering what's underneath while providing
        room to move and play and see.
      </p>

      <h3>Serendipity</h3>
      <p>
        I was moving some of these forms around and digitally stumbled onto a layout that just
        worked. I ran with it &amp; am really — strangely — pleased &amp; excited about these
        arrangements.
      </p>
      <p>
        In the end, I don't know where I'd consistently use any of this. I work at Crema; I'm not
        a freelancer doing brand/identity work; I don't really need a Personal Brand™ to this
        degree. It's been a fun exploration &amp; explanation exercise, though, one that gets me
        outside of my product-centric tendencies.
      </p>

      <p class="muted small">
        Originally posted on <a href="https://medium.com/@thehilker/making-my-own-mark-d11488090287">Medium</a>, June 2019.
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>

<style>
  main {
    animation: fadeIn 0.4s ease-out;
  }

  @keyframes fadeIn {
    from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(8px); }
    to   { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }
  }

  header {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .back {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .back:hover {
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  header p {
    margin-top: 0.25rem;
  }

  article :global(img) {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: 4px;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }

  .caption {
    margin-top: -1rem;
    text-align: center;
  }
</style>
